1
00:01:43,846 --> 00:01:49,846
Olive, the Other Reindeer is a animation, family,
comedy film directed by Steve Moore.

2
00:03:27,692 --> 00:03:33,692
After mis-hearing a radio broadcast about Santa canceling his Christmas trip due to an injured
reindeer, Olive the dog decides to travel to the North Pole and volunteer to be a replacement.

3
00:05:11,538 --> 00:05:17,538
Unfortunately, Olive must deal with an evil postman who's
tired of handling all the extra Christmas mail.

4
00:06:55,384 --> 00:07:01,384
Will Martini the penguin be able to help Olive realize her dream?

5
00:08:39,230 --> 00:08:45,230
Olive (voice), played by Drew Barrymore.

6
00:10:23,076 --> 00:10:29,076
Santa Claus (voice), played by Ed Asner.

7
00:12:06,923 --> 00:12:12,923
The Postman (voice), played by Dan Castellaneta.

8
00:13:50,769 --> 00:13:56,769
Martini (voice), played by Joe Pantoliano.

9
00:15:34,615 --> 00:15:40,615
Fido (voice), played by Peter MacNicol.

10
00:17:18,461 --> 00:17:24,461
Arturo (voice), played by Matt Groening.

11
00:19:02,307 --> 00:19:08,307
Olive, the Other Reindeer is a 1999 American animated Christmas comedy musical television special
written by Steve Young, based on the 1997 children's book by Vivian Walsh and J.

12
00:20:46,153 --> 00:20:52,153
Otto Seibold, and directed by Academy Award-nominated
animator Steve Moore (credited as "Oscar Moore").

13
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:36,000
The feature was produced by Matt Groening's The Curiosity
Company and animated by DNA Productions.

14
00:24:13,846 --> 00:24:19,846
Drew Barrymore voices the title character, and she is
credited as an executive producer on the special.

15
00:25:57,692 --> 00:26:03,692
The special first aired on December 17, 1999, alongside Futurama on Fox, exactly ten
years after the premiere date of Groening's television series The Simpsons.

16
00:27:41,538 --> 00:27:47,538
It was produced by Fox Television Studios and Flower Films.

17
00:29:25,384 --> 00:29:31,384
During its initial broadcast, the special brought in 6.06 million
viewers, and an additional 5.22 million the following year.

18
00:31:09,230 --> 00:31:15,230
After airing again the following year, the special made its first cable television premiere on Nickelodeon on December 15, 2001; It would then premiere the
following year on Cartoon Network on December 14, 2002, and aired during each holiday season until it was last seen on December 24, 2012.

19
00:32:53,076 --> 00:32:59,076
The special would also air on other local syndicated networks,
such as The WB, MyNetworkTV, The CW and WGN-TV.

20
00:34:36,923 --> 00:34:42,923
The story is based on the 1997 children's book by Vivian Walsh and J.

21
00:36:20,769 --> 00:36:26,769
Otto Seibold and illustrated by J.

22
00:38:04,615 --> 00:38:10,615
Otto Seibold.

23
00:39:48,461 --> 00:39:54,461
In the song, "Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer", the lyric "All of the other reindeer" can be misheard
in dialects with the cot–caught merger as the mondegreen "Olive, the other reindeer".

24
00:41:32,307 --> 00:41:38,307
The special was nominated for the Emmy Award for Outstanding Animated Program.

25
00:43:16,153 --> 00:43:22,153
The tone is lighthearted.
